The cupidos-txt/ directory is the canonical source for the shipped manuals.
Cupid OS embeds every cupidos-txt/*.CTXT file into ramfs as /docs/*.ctxt, then
renders them inside Notepad as live DolDoc-style manuals.

>sprite /home/snail.bmp 64 64 | open:11CTXT.CTXT
>button Open Docs Index | open:00INDEX.CTXT
>button List /docs | shell:ls /docs
>button List /home | shell:ls /home
>button Open DolDoc Manual | open:11CTXT.CTXT

>h2 Canonical Path
>tree open Canonical Path
Canonical authoring path:
- repo source: cupidos-txt/*.CTXT
- boot-time install: /docs/*.ctxt
- renderer host: Notepad render mode

Workflow:
1) Edit the source file in cupidos-txt/
2) Build CupidOS so the file is embedded into ramfs
3) Open /docs/00INDEX.ctxt or any /docs/*.ctxt page in Notepad
4) Press F2 to switch between source and rendered DolDoc view

The source remains readable as text. Rendered view adds working buttons,
runnable CupidC, action links, trees, and inline BMP, PNG, or JPEG widgets.
>endtree

>h2 Makefile Auto-Discovery
>tree open Makefile Auto-Discovery
The Makefile auto-discovers and embeds these source sets:

  bin/*.cc     -> kernel/util/bin_programs_gen.cc  -> /bin/*.cc in ramfs
  bin/*.h      -> kernel/util/bin_programs_gen.cc  -> /bin/*.h in ramfs
  bin/browser/*.cc -> kernel/util/bin_programs_gen.cc -> /bin/browser/*.cc
  cupidos-txt/*.CTXT -> kernel/util/docs_programs_gen.cc -> /docs/*.ctxt
  *.bmp/*.png/*.jpg/*.jpeg -> kernel/util/docs_programs_gen.cc -> /home/<same name>
  demos/*.asm  -> kernel/util/demos_programs_gen.cc -> /demos/*.asm in ramfs
  god/*.DD     -> wrapped by CupidObj         -> /god/*.DD in image

CupidObj uses its text wrapper for every source set above. CRLF pairs become
LF in the wrapped object, while lone carriage returns stay intact. This keeps
the installed files and kernel layout stable across Windows and Linux
checkouts. Images, fonts, and other binary inputs still use byte-exact wrap.

CupidObj also understands the three generated installation-table formats.
Its install-source command validates bin, docs, home-asset, browser, header,
and demo path groups before it writes a .cc table. Repeated generation is
byte-identical, and a failed request leaves the previous table intact. The
checked seed applies one overflow-safe limit of 512 paths across every group
before it dispatches a mode. It also keeps caller order when home assets mix
extensions.
Before writing a table, CupidObj compares the complete wrapped symbol for
every entry. Distinct paths that collapse to one linked symbol are rejected.
The exact same BMP may appear once in the docs list and once in the home list
because both entries use the same wrapped object.
The Python oracle follows the same order. The command is self-hosted and
present in the checked toolchain seed. The current image build runs checked
CupidObj for all three tables. Its active inventories remain within the limit
and keep the same bytes. The recipes depend on the complete checked-seed trust
unit.
The Python generator remains a byte-parity oracle but no longer owns these
production sources. ADR 0204 records the transfer, and ADR 0206 records the
first seed with the linked-symbol collision checks. ADR 0234 records the
current checked seed.

Makefile variable definitions:
  BIN_CC_SRCS  := $(filter-out bin/old_cc2.cc bin/old_cc2_single.cc, $(wildcard bin/*.cc))
  BROWSER_SUB_SRCS := $(wildcard bin/browser/*.cc)
  DOC_CTXT_SRCS := $(wildcard cupidos-txt/*.CTXT)
  HOME_ASSET_SRCS := $(HOME_BMP_SRCS) $(HOME_PNG_SRCS) $(HOME_JPG_SRCS) $(HOME_JPEG_SRCS)
  DEMO_ASM_SRCS := $(wildcard demos/*.asm)
  GOD_DD_SRCS   := $(wildcard god/*.DD)

The next build finds new .CTXT files in cupidos-txt/ through
docs_programs_gen.cc. It also embeds new top-level .bmp, .png, .jpg, and .jpeg
files, which the boot path installs under /home with their original names.
Checked CupidObj accepts sequential SOF0 or SOF1 JPEG files and rejects
progressive, unsupported, or malformed input. Python checks accepted bytes
independently before the object is published.

bin_programs_gen.cc likewise finds new .cc files under bin/.

The god/*.DD files are wrapped by CupidObj pattern rules; they are available at
runtime under /god/ for godspeak, oracle, and bible commands.
>endtree

>h2 Method A: Homefs Persistent Docs (/home)
>tree open Method A: Homefs Persistent Docs
Goal:
- Copy docs under /home when you want user-editable or persistent local copies.

Current image model:
- cupidos.img is a single HDD image.
- /home is the persistent writable filesystem inside cupidos.img (homefs).

Suggested target layout:
  /home/docs/00INDEX.CTXT
  /home/docs/01SHELL.CTXT
  ...

Notes:
- /docs/*.ctxt is still the shipped canonical manual set.
- /home/docs is best treated as a user copy or scratch area.
- Keeping the .CTXT extension preserves Notepad render-mode detection.

Simple in-OS approach:
1) Make directory:
   mkdir /home/docs
2) Create files one-by-one with ed and paste content.
3) Run sync after edits.

Host copy approach (recommended for bulk docs):
  mcopy -o -i cupidos.img@@10485760 00INDEX.CTXT ::/00INDEX.CTXT
  mcopy -o -i cupidos.img@@10485760 01SHELL.CTXT ::/01SHELL.CTXT
  mdir  -i cupidos.img@@10485760 ::/

Mapping reminder:
- FAT ::/ is always mounted as CupidOS /disk.
- On a fresh image with no HOMEFS.SYS, the first homefs mount imports FAT-root
  files into /home once. Later host-staged files remain under /disk until an
  in-guest copy places them in /home.

If you open a copied .CTXT file in Notepad, the same DolDoc-lite renderer
still applies as long as the filename keeps the .ctxt or .CTXT suffix.
>endtree

>h2 Method B: RAMFS Memory Docs (/)
>tree open Method B: RAMFS Memory Docs
Goal:
- Bundle small docs directly in kernel boot memory files.

This older approach remains available for manually bundled files. Shipped
manuals should use the generated /docs/*.ctxt path instead of hardcoded text
blobs in kernel/fs/fs.cc.

Tradeoff:
- Always available at boot
- Non-persistent (rebuilt image required)
>endtree

>h2 Method C: Auto-Install Docs And Images At Boot (Advanced)
>tree open Method C: Auto-Install Docs And Images At Boot
Pattern is already implemented for:
- /bin programs (bin/*.cc)
- /docs pages (cupidos-txt/*.CTXT)
- /demos asm sources (demos/*.asm)
- /docs/image.bmp sprite assets
- /home image assets (*.bmp, *.png, *.jpg, *.jpeg at repo root)
- /god/*.DD word lists (via CupidObj)

Build-time generators:
- kernel/util/bin_programs_gen.cc
- kernel/util/docs_programs_gen.cc
- kernel/util/demos_programs_gen.cc

Tradeoff:
- Avoids hardcoded strings in kernel/fs/fs.cc
- Requires Makefile and kernel generation changes

>button List /docs | shell:ls /docs
>button Open 00INDEX | open:00INDEX.CTXT
>endtree

>h2 Practical Recommendation
>tree open Practical Recommendation
For shipped system manuals:
1) Keep canonical docs in cupidos-txt/
2) Rebuild so they install to /docs/*.ctxt
3) Browse them in Notepad render mode

For user-customized copies:
1) Copy selected .CTXT files into /home/docs
2) Edit them there
3) Keep the .CTXT extension so render mode still works

For asm demos used in docs:
- Use make sync-demos to refresh /home/demos in cupidos.img.

For image assets used in docs:
- Put BMP, PNG, JPG, or JPEG files at the repo root
- Rebuild so /home/<same filename> is available to >sprite, >image, and >img
- Top-level JPG/JPEG sources must use sequential SOF0 or SOF1 encoding
- Progressive, unsupported, or malformed input stops the build with a useful
  diagnostic and leaves the previous object unchanged
>endtree

